We have a favorite first question of all of our guests.

125
00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:27.000
Okay, here it is.

126
00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:28.040
It is.

127
00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:29.000
It's really hard.

128
00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:29.880
It's really hard.

129
00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:30.200
Ready?

130
00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:32.740
How did you get your job on The Office?

131
00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:34.540
I auditioned.

132
00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:39.740
I have to say the audition for The Office was unlike any audition I've ever had.

133
00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:44.960
And I'm sure you had the same experience because it felt like it was a workshop.

134
00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:47.660
It didn't feel like an audition.

135
00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:49.540
There didn't seem to be a ton of pressure.

136
00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:57.940
You just went in and they had a camera, but you didn't think there was anyone outside of

137
00:05:57.940 --> 00:06:00.880
the, you know, the one or two people that were in the room.

138
00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:04.180
So it was very low-keyed and very supportive.

139
00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:11.500
I remember we did a screen test together because after I auditioned something like seven times,

140
00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:13.700
I made it to the end, I guess.

141
00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:15.700
And I was like, I was one of the final four.

142
00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:18.840
And they paired me up with you for a couple of scenes.

143
00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:20.360
That was crazy.

144
00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:22.280
I'd never done something like that before.

145
00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:24.160
I said, I'd anybody but her.

146
00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:25.500
You're like, I don't care.

147
00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:26.360
No chemistry.

148
00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:27.140
What is her name?

149
00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:27.660
Gemma?

150
00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:29.540
Gemma Fisker.

151
00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:30.700
I don't like it.

152
00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:32.240
Don't think it's going to work.

153
00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:34.080
I don't think Gemma Fisker is going to be on the show.

154
00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:41.460
No, I remember auditioning with you and I remember how grounded you were instantly in

155
00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:42.040
your character.

156
00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:45.680
And I thought, you know, when you read with someone, you don't know who they are.

157
00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:46.640
As a person.

158
00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:46.960
Yeah.

159
00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:50.360
And you immediately think, well, that's who she is, clearly.

160
00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:54.380
Because you can't separate it at that point.

161
00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:59.600
But then you find out, like, everyone has completely different personalities than the

162
00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:01.380
characters are portraying.

163
00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:02.520
And it's kind of amazing.

164
00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:04.320
Like, once I remember.

165
00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:05.820
Can I tell a story?

166
00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:06.780
Yes, yes.

167
00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:07.380
Oh, my gosh.

168
00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:14.120
I remember when we were at our first studio, our first soundstage out in Culver City, and

169
00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:22.740
we were shooting the pilot, and I was at craft service, and I had taken a can of Diet Coke or

170
00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:24.360
something out of the six-pack.

171
00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:28.640
And, you know, there was that plastic, the carrying six-pack thing.

172
00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:30.440
And I started breaking it down.

173
00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:33.340
I started cutting the edges before I threw it in the trash.

174
00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:33.640
Yes.

175
00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:34.700
You know, because of the dolphins.

176
00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:35.000
Right.

177
00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:41.200
And I remember you walking up and commending me for doing that.

178
00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:47.080
And that stuck with me, like, oh, yeah, I was just kind of doing it as a courtesy to

179
00:07:47.080 --> 00:07:48.300
the dolphins, of course.

180
00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:55.080
And I remember you kind of giving me a pat on the back and saying, hey, good for you.

181
00:07:55.300 --> 00:07:55.800
Nice job.

182
00:07:56.060 --> 00:07:58.820
That is such a specific memory to remember.

183
00:07:59.100 --> 00:07:59.520
I did.

184
00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:04.860
Like, it's one of my strongest memories from the first episode.

185
00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:06.880
And I thought, she's so nice.

186
00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:10.200
That's so, it's so nice to be with nice people.

187
00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:11.360
I guess that was the takeaway.

188
00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:13.240
Like, oh, this is going to be great.

189
00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:14.540
Hello, dolphin lover.

190
00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:17.200
You know, I do that at home.

191
00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:18.760
I probably said I do that at home.

192
00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:19.020
Yeah.

193
00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:20.240
Yeah, I love to see it.

194
00:08:20.340 --> 00:08:23.380
And we should shout out to all the people out there.

195
00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:24.280
Cut the plastic.

196
00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:25.120
Cut the plastic.

197
00:08:25.340 --> 00:08:25.980
Don't, you know.

198
00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:26.340
Yeah.

199
00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:28.320
Keep the plastic down.

200
00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:29.100
Yes.

201
00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:30.500
That's right.

202
00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:32.600
This is going to be a PSA.

203
00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:39.620
It is so funny, though, like where your brain goes, because my strongest memory of the pilot

204
00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:45.540
is how you had to go down those stone steps outside to get like to crafting everything,

205
00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:49.880
you know, like you had to go outside the building down these stone staircase.

206
00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:52.320
And Oscar and I were leaving at the same time.

207
00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:55.860
And we had done sketch comedy together for years, right?

208
00:08:55.860 --> 00:09:01.360
So we just went back to our silly shtick and he goes, let's walk down the stairs together

209
00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:02.840
the same way.

210
00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:05.020
And I said, OK, Oscar.

211
00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:08.260
And we started to go down the stairs with our arms flinging about.

212
00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:15.120
And we turned and the whole like producing team was walking behind us, like Terry Weinberg,

213
00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:16.780
Howard Klein, all of those people.

214
00:09:16.780 --> 00:09:19.800
And I was like, Oscar, there's a bunch of people behind us.

215
00:09:19.900 --> 00:09:21.420
He goes, then we have to keep doing it.

216
00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:24.920
Yeah, you have to own it.

217
00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:25.240
Yeah.

218
00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:27.820
So I'm like, OK, that's that's the one thing I remember.

219
00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:32.520
But then you find out later on those intimidating people weren't intimidating at all.

220
00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:36.480
Everyone was completely so nice and rooting for us.

221
00:09:36.580 --> 00:09:36.700
Yeah.

222
00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:38.000
Everyone's in the same boat.

223
00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:42.140
I have a question that came from our Office Ladies fan group.

224
00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:43.800
This is from Carly D.

225
00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:48.840
Carly would like to know, what was your process and inspiration behind playing Michael?

226
00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:51.040
How did you craft this character?

227
00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:59.260
I remember meeting with Greg Daniels up at a deli, like on the top of Mulholland.

228
00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:01.660
And we didn't know each other at all.

229
00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:04.560
And it's before it's before I got the part, actually.

230
00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:09.240
He, you know, he was I think he was meeting with some of the people who were sort of in

231
00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:11.620
the mix, the final mix for Michael.

232
00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:19.320
And we just discussed the character and talked about what his foibles are and what is his

233
00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:20.520
dreams and hopes.

234
00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:24.560
And and I had a pretty specific take at that point.

235
00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:30.140
And it wasn't Ricky Gervais's, you know, and I and I've told this story a million times

236
00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:34.480
that I chose not to watch the British version because I just didn't want that.

237
00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:38.140
to influence whatever this version was going to be.

238
00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:42.160
And because he was so he was clearly so great at it.

239
00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:43.600
It was such a distinctive character.

240
00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:46.120
I didn't want to do an impression of him.

241
00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:47.700
I remember you telling me that.

242
00:10:47.900 --> 00:10:53.100
And I remember telling you that I had chosen to watch the entire British series and completely

243
00:10:53.100 --> 00:11:00.020
copy Lucy Davis and was basically doing a Lucy Davis impression to get the job.

244
00:11:00.460 --> 00:11:00.940
So I really.

245
00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:01.480
So not true.

246
00:11:01.660 --> 00:11:01.740
Yeah.

247
00:11:02.060 --> 00:11:02.300
So.

248
00:11:04.260 --> 00:11:05.480
But also kind of true.

249
00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:07.140
I don't think so.

250
00:11:07.400 --> 00:11:11.800
Until until, you know, especially for the pilot, because we were doing it word for word.

251
00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:13.520
And I was like, I think she nailed it.

252
00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:15.560
Like, I don't know how to say the line better than that.

253
00:11:15.660 --> 00:11:16.660
So I guess I'm just going to go with.

254
00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:17.140
See, I never.

255
00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:17.660
What she did.

256
00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:18.040
Well.

257
00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:21.760
But I remember you saying you'd never seen it and I didn't really have information in

258
00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:22.100
your brain.

259
00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:28.920
I didn't because, you know, I watched like a minute of just to get a sense of the tone

260
00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:36.820
of the show, sort of how how dark and very dry, very dour, kind of bleak, which I loved.

261
00:11:36.900 --> 00:11:39.260
I loved how still it was at times.

262
00:11:39.260 --> 00:11:45.140
But his character was so specific, I thought, no, I can't, because that's what I'll want

263
00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:45.460
to do.

264
00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:47.120
I won't I won't see it any other way.

265
00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:53.240
And I think Greg agreed with that as sort of a template for doing it.

266
00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:59.540
And we were definitely on the same page in terms of who this guy could be and could become.

267
00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:07.300
And and I early on, I started thinking about an arc to like where we're because you hope,

268
00:12:07.300 --> 00:12:11.780
you know, you don't go in thinking this is just going to be a pilot or this is just going

269
00:12:11.780 --> 00:12:12.720
to be six episodes.

270
00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:18.460
The hope is the character starts here and ends up way somewhere else.

271
00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:25.300
There's no way of really knowing where characters going to to end in the last episode.

272
00:12:25.340 --> 00:12:32.260
But but I kind of in my mind, I had a plan like because these characters, the hope was that all

273
00:12:32.260 --> 00:12:34.980
of these characters would change and evolve and grow.

274
00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:41.880
And and that's part of what's fun about watching a show is to see progress in these characters.

275
00:12:42.280 --> 00:12:45.000
So that was that was sort of the plan going in.

276
00:12:45.140 --> 00:12:45.980
That was sort of the hope.

277
00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:51.500
It was one of those parts that you just pray that you get because.

278
00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:58.700
Well, you know, at that point, certainly we were all just happy to be working.

279
00:12:58.700 --> 00:12:59.200
Yeah.

280
00:12:59.400 --> 00:12:59.500
Yeah.

281
00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:05.580
But on top of it, it just seemed like what a what a perfect thing to be a part

282
00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:05.820
of.

283
00:13:06.220 --> 00:13:06.500
Yeah.

284
00:13:06.580 --> 00:13:13.740
And everyone who I met who had been involved with it seemed great and smart and nice

285
00:13:13.740 --> 00:13:14.660
and kind.

286
00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:18.780
And all of those things just made it so appealing to to want to be.

287
00:13:18.920 --> 00:13:20.700
I was, you know, crossing my fingers.

288
00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:21.720
Oh, I know.

289
00:13:21.720 --> 00:13:26.080
I remember thinking like I can do this like this one I can do.

290
00:13:26.080 --> 00:13:26.540
Yeah.

291
00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:28.640
But that was Pam and I didn't get it.

292
00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:35.500
But then I got to be the accountant and then I was like, no, I can really do this.

293
00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:42.460
Have you ever worked in an office in real life or ever had a boss like Michael Scott?

294
00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:44.040
No, no, no.

295
00:13:44.300 --> 00:13:44.980
I never have.

296
00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:46.440
Not an office worker.

297
00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:49.260
I did a lot of, you know, I did a lot of waiting tables.

298
00:13:49.940 --> 00:13:53.200
I sold wine over the phone at one point.

299
00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:54.920
How do you sell wine over the phone?

300
00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:55.440
Exactly.

301
00:13:57.320 --> 00:13:59.240
I mean, you can't taste it.

302
00:13:59.380 --> 00:13:59.980
How does it?

303
00:14:00.180 --> 00:14:01.740
It's a beautiful bottle.

304
00:14:01.820 --> 00:14:04.940
Well, you describe it based on but we didn't get to drink the wine.

305
00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:11.080
But you have to describe a wine based on a, you know, a paragraph that they've written on each

306
00:14:11.080 --> 00:14:15.200
on each wine and varietal characteristics of each wine.

307
00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:17.260
It's a hard sell.

308
00:14:18.140 --> 00:14:18.660
Hello.

309
00:14:19.320 --> 00:14:20.520
Do you like wine?

310
00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:21.000
I mean.

311
00:14:21.140 --> 00:14:21.280
Click.

312
00:14:21.280 --> 00:14:21.380
Click.

313
00:14:25.940 --> 00:14:26.540
Okay.

314
00:14:26.820 --> 00:14:29.340
Was the office your first lead role?

315
00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:32.720
Yeah, I think it was.

316
00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:34.220
I had done.

317
00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:37.480
It was a, it was really fun to do.

318
00:14:37.480 --> 00:14:44.600
I, but I played a crazy Greek chef on a sitcom with Annie Potts and Tim Curry.

319
00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:49.280
So that's, that's the, that's the sitcom that I had done.

320
00:14:49.280 --> 00:14:57.320
And I did a, I did a show just before the office starring Tom Papa, a really funny comedian.

321
00:14:57.320 --> 00:15:02.200
And he had a show come to Papa and he was, he was so great.

322
00:15:02.380 --> 00:15:05.440
And, and the show, you know, only lasted a few episodes.

323
00:15:05.460 --> 00:15:12.660
So I, I went from that and then auditioned for the office when that, when that ended, but no, I'd never done a lead.

324
00:15:12.820 --> 00:15:13.980
I'd never done a lead role.

325
00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:20.620
Cause I, I have such a memory of you, Steve, sitting over on that little sofa at front reception or the chair.

326
00:15:20.780 --> 00:15:27.280
Sometimes that was in front of Michael's office, just studying your lines because you, you would have to carry every scene.

327
00:15:27.280 --> 00:15:32.880
And I just breezed through and said something snarky to Kevin and then breezed out, you know, for the most part.

328
00:15:33.040 --> 00:15:38.400
But I, I always thought, was there a lot of pressure to be the lead and have to carry all of that?

329
00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:44.440
Not, not, not with that group of people, because I felt like everyone was an equal component of that show.

330
00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:45.400
I didn't feel that way.

331
00:15:45.500 --> 00:15:51.300
I didn't feel like I was carrying any more load than anyone else.

332
00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:58.640
Because when you think about it, you know, yeah, I had a lot of dialogue, but everyone was there all the time.

333
00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:04.260
And even if you were in the background of a scene, you're still acting, you're still active.

334
00:16:04.260 --> 00:16:10.800
You, like, all of us had character stuff going on at all times.

335
00:16:10.900 --> 00:16:22.600
So I, I don't think enough can be said for the fact that to be, to just be present on that set, everyone was working really hard.

336
00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:26.740
But it didn't, it didn't, I don't, I don't want to make it sound like it was hard work because it wasn't.

337
00:16:26.800 --> 00:16:27.860
It was really fun.

338
00:16:28.040 --> 00:16:28.320
Right.

339
00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:30.400
Because everyone was there supporting each other.

340
00:16:30.400 --> 00:16:34.660
So no, I never felt, I never felt like any undue pressure.

341
00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:50.420
And also the writing was so good that at a certain point it became very easy to memorize lines because you started to understand exactly how the writers were writing it and how they were also writing to us,

342
00:16:50.680 --> 00:16:53.300
like to our characters and to all of our voices.

343
00:16:54.320 --> 00:17:00.720
So I find that if dialogue's really good, it's generally pretty easy to remember because it makes sense.

344
00:17:00.860 --> 00:17:01.400
That's right.

345
00:17:01.400 --> 00:17:02.960
I always think about that too.

346
00:17:03.100 --> 00:17:04.680
If it's well-written, I have no problem.

347
00:17:04.900 --> 00:17:07.720
And then our joke is we'd never make it on a medical show.

348
00:17:09.500 --> 00:17:13.500
Because all of the things they have to just quickly rapid fire off.

349
00:17:13.600 --> 00:17:16.600
I'd be like, oh no, I'm not, I'm not going to audition for that.

350
00:17:16.820 --> 00:17:18.020
It'll be a disaster.

351
00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:18.440
Yeah.

352
00:17:18.700 --> 00:17:19.520
No, that would be tough.

353
00:17:19.520 --> 00:17:34.640
So Steve, we have talked about many times on this podcast, just about our gratitude for your just stability and consistency and your leadership.

354
00:17:34.640 --> 00:17:42.300
So much so that a fan wrote in Sarah H and said, we've heard how supportive and professional Steve is on set.

355
00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:48.660
And I would love to hear Steve's perspective or advice on setting the tone at work and fostering a supportive environment.

356
00:17:48.660 --> 00:17:51.380
Let's start by saying, I am pretty great.

357
00:17:53.460 --> 00:17:55.380
So some of it's just in your DNA.

358
00:17:55.380 --> 00:17:58.760
I mean, you had a shirt you would wear that said, I'm fantastic, right?

359
00:17:59.240 --> 00:17:59.960
That's right.

360
00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:01.740
A shirt with my face on it.

361
00:18:01.940 --> 00:18:04.780
So just to reiterate that it's me.

362
00:18:05.520 --> 00:18:06.980
That's really nice of you to say.

363
00:18:07.840 --> 00:18:13.360
But again, I felt like I was part of this very unique ensemble.

364
00:18:13.360 --> 00:18:16.820
And so I never thought of myself that way.

365
00:18:17.040 --> 00:18:23.660
I thought of Greg as our leader and our sort of mentor in terms of this world.

366
00:18:23.880 --> 00:18:26.320
And he oversaw everything.

367
00:18:26.900 --> 00:18:29.320
And everyone was so nice.

368
00:18:29.540 --> 00:18:36.700
Everyone was so kind to each other that I never got a sense that it should be any other way than the,

369
00:18:36.700 --> 00:18:48.440
just in terms of the production of the show, like from wardrobe to the writers, to all of the crew, you know, catering, craft service.

370
00:18:48.640 --> 00:18:50.860
Everybody felt like they were a part of something.

371
00:18:50.860 --> 00:19:03.520
And the other part of it is, I believe sincerely that every one of us knew, no matter who watched it or whether it had any sort of longevity in terms of viewership,

372
00:19:03.740 --> 00:19:08.580
even if, you know, if we got canceled after a season or two or three, I was so,

373
00:19:08.700 --> 00:19:17.320
I think we were all so happy and thankful for the time that we got to work on the show and to be with one another.

374
00:19:17.320 --> 00:19:20.820
It was really, it just, it was a special, a special thing.

375
00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:24.020
And we were aware of how special it was in the moment, I think.

376
00:19:24.420 --> 00:19:25.160
For sure.

377
00:19:25.380 --> 00:19:25.920
For sure.

378
00:19:26.320 --> 00:19:38.100
I always was just super thankful for the writing because it was like, you know, when you do an improv show or a sketch comedy show or something,

379
00:19:38.180 --> 00:19:42.660
and there's one person that wants the joke, wants the joke, wants the joke, and they just kill the show.

380
00:19:43.060 --> 00:19:44.920
I never felt that on our show.

381
00:19:44.920 --> 00:19:51.040
It was like, if Phyllis had the best button, she could level the room or Oscar or Creed.

382
00:19:51.200 --> 00:19:51.800
It didn't matter.

383
00:19:52.180 --> 00:19:54.100
Like the jokes came from everyone.

384
00:19:54.300 --> 00:19:55.940
It was a real creative collective.

385
00:19:56.020 --> 00:19:57.560
And the writers wrote that way.

386
00:19:57.720 --> 00:19:59.160
And Greg treated us that way.

387
00:19:59.480 --> 00:20:02.040
Like Greg could look at a scene and be like, no, you know what?

388
00:20:02.120 --> 00:20:04.460
It's not working with Jim getting the laugh.

389
00:20:04.840 --> 00:20:07.820
Let's flip that and let's have Stanley say it.

390
00:20:07.940 --> 00:20:08.080
Right.

391
00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:09.680
And then it just floored everyone.

392
00:20:09.680 --> 00:20:11.620
And so I always felt that way.

393
00:20:11.660 --> 00:20:15.280
And I feel like Greg set that tone for us that like everybody gets to play.

394
00:20:15.460 --> 00:20:16.840
You're exactly right.

395
00:20:17.840 --> 00:20:19.860
Everyone got their shot.

396
00:20:20.100 --> 00:20:23.500
Not just that, but everyone else loved it.

397
00:20:23.620 --> 00:20:25.220
Everyone else was supportive of it.

398
00:20:25.360 --> 00:20:28.580
There was no resentment, no ego involved.

399
00:20:29.600 --> 00:20:31.260
And like, why not?

400
00:20:31.440 --> 00:20:32.380
That's the way to do it.

401
00:20:32.380 --> 00:20:37.620
And I think you can see it when you watch the show because there is a calm to it.

402
00:20:37.780 --> 00:20:41.180
Like no one's, everyone, it's all good.

403
00:20:41.700 --> 00:20:41.960
Yeah.

404
00:20:42.320 --> 00:20:47.640
Everyone's, everyone is, everyone's loving each other, you know, in terms of us as actors

405
00:20:47.640 --> 00:20:50.360
and people beyond the characters.

406
00:20:50.660 --> 00:20:54.760
I get so excited for conference room scenes because those were our rapid fire things.

407
00:20:55.100 --> 00:21:00.240
I mean, Michael is up there, but literally every single person in the room has either

408
00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:04.020
a bit or a look, and it's just so fun to watch.

409
00:21:04.180 --> 00:21:05.580
Almost impossible to get through.

410
00:21:05.800 --> 00:21:07.300
Oh, we, and we didn't.

411
00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:08.640
Oh my God, we laughed so much.

412
00:21:08.860 --> 00:21:13.860
Well, people are very excited for you to talk about some of your favorite memories of shooting

413
00:21:13.860 --> 00:21:14.300
The Office.

414
00:21:15.100 --> 00:21:19.040
Specifically, they're curious if you have a favorite episode, which I think is like asking,

415
00:21:19.180 --> 00:21:20.020
do you have a favorite child?

416
00:21:20.140 --> 00:21:20.840
It's very hard.

417
00:21:20.840 --> 00:21:21.320
It's so hard.

418
00:21:21.620 --> 00:21:22.920
Yeah, that is hard.

419
00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:26.900
You know, my last one, talk about heart.

420
00:21:26.900 --> 00:21:33.700
It was a really difficult episode to do, but I also loved it at the same time because

421
00:21:34.300 --> 00:21:37.480
it was sort of that end game for Michael.

422
00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:45.000
It was that the culmination, the sort of showing the growth that he didn't need the big send

423
00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:45.160
off.

424
00:21:45.240 --> 00:21:46.500
He didn't need the big party.

425
00:21:46.720 --> 00:21:54.240
He could say goodbye to all of his friends on his own terms without any of the, you know,

426
00:21:54.380 --> 00:21:55.160
the fanfare.

427
00:21:55.160 --> 00:21:55.540
Exactly.

428
00:21:56.200 --> 00:22:03.620
And, and so that, that I, I really enjoyed, you know, having that character evolve to that

429
00:22:03.620 --> 00:22:04.620
point in that moment.

430
00:22:04.780 --> 00:22:11.520
And people always point to this one, but, um, but dinner party, dinner party's up there

431
00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:15.480
and, you know, we'd come back after the writer's strike.

432
00:22:15.620 --> 00:22:17.340
So that was the first thing that we had shot.

433
00:22:17.460 --> 00:22:22.780
I know you've discussed it, but I think everyone was so ready to get back to work and had really

434
00:22:22.780 --> 00:22:23.560
missed each other.

435
00:22:23.740 --> 00:22:29.840
And this is an episode that had just been sitting there waiting to happen for a while.

436
00:22:30.500 --> 00:22:40.740
And it's so weird and, and kind of dense in thematically and crazy and had great guest

437
00:22:40.740 --> 00:22:43.860
appearances and great site gags.

438
00:22:43.860 --> 00:22:50.860
It was also really dark in a lot of ways and very sad and kind of emotionally fraught.

439
00:22:51.120 --> 00:22:53.900
So I, that, there was a lot going on in that one.

440
00:22:54.000 --> 00:22:55.260
So I, I liked that one too.

441
00:22:55.440 --> 00:22:59.000
We didn't realize until we were breaking it down and we did a little Googling.

442
00:22:59.000 --> 00:23:01.980
Apparently it was a huge bomb when it first aired.

443
00:23:02.320 --> 00:23:04.060
It was not a fan favorite.

444
00:23:04.440 --> 00:23:09.380
It is not what it is today, but it was, um, people did not care for it.

445
00:23:09.520 --> 00:23:14.660
The show in general, you know, was a huge bomb when people first saw it.

446
00:23:14.780 --> 00:23:17.820
So I, you know, acquired taste, I suppose.

447
00:23:18.300 --> 00:23:21.980
Another one to your point that people did not like at first was Scott's Tots.

448
00:23:22.100 --> 00:23:22.280
Yes.

449
00:23:22.280 --> 00:23:31.100
And I loved doing that episode because it was so horrible, but, but I think to express so much

450
00:23:31.100 --> 00:23:40.140
about this guy's persona because his heart definitely in the right place and a huge heart, but so wrong.

451
00:23:40.360 --> 00:23:41.440
No follow through.

452
00:23:41.720 --> 00:23:48.300
And I think there was a talking head, something to the effect of, of all the stupid things

453
00:23:48.300 --> 00:23:49.580
I've done in my life.

454
00:23:49.580 --> 00:23:56.140
If this was the most generous or something like that, it was something to that effect.

455
00:23:56.720 --> 00:23:59.720
I think it's like of all the empty promises I've made in my life.

456
00:23:59.900 --> 00:24:01.080
This one was the most generous.

457
00:24:03.300 --> 00:24:04.420
Great memory, Jenna.

458
00:24:04.780 --> 00:24:05.320
That's it.

459
00:24:05.620 --> 00:24:08.260
That's just such a hard line to say with a straight face.

460
00:24:09.020 --> 00:24:11.440
You have so many amazing talking heads.

461
00:24:11.600 --> 00:24:16.240
We talk about it every week and I sort of go and look at the deleted scenes and there's

462
00:24:16.240 --> 00:24:17.540
some real gems in there too.

463
00:24:17.540 --> 00:24:19.620
But Jenna, your favorite is...

464
00:24:19.620 --> 00:24:20.380
I have a favorite.

465
00:24:20.620 --> 00:24:20.760
Yeah.

466
00:24:20.980 --> 00:24:24.100
And we have an audio clip of it so that we can listen to it together.

467
00:24:24.380 --> 00:24:24.600
Okay.

468
00:24:24.820 --> 00:24:27.600
Is after Michael burns his foot on the Foreman grill.

469
00:24:27.820 --> 00:24:28.440
Oh, yeah.

470
00:24:28.440 --> 00:24:33.200
And you very angrily explain how it happened.

471
00:24:33.920 --> 00:24:35.240
You're so annoyed.

472
00:24:35.460 --> 00:24:36.720
You're so defensive.

473
00:24:37.060 --> 00:24:39.200
Like, why do I have to explain this?

474
00:24:39.360 --> 00:24:40.660
But I guess here it goes.

475
00:24:41.020 --> 00:24:41.460
Again.

476
00:24:42.020 --> 00:24:43.020
Wait, we got to hear it.

477
00:24:43.020 --> 00:24:45.240
I enjoy having breakfast in bed.

478
00:24:45.420 --> 00:24:47.300
I like waking up to the smell of bacon.

479
00:24:47.500 --> 00:24:47.780
Sue me.

480
00:24:48.140 --> 00:24:50.720
And since I don't have a butler, I have to do it myself.

481
00:24:51.180 --> 00:24:56.880
So, most nights before I go to bed, I will lay six strips of bacon out on my George Foreman

482
00:24:56.880 --> 00:24:57.160
grill.

483
00:24:57.600 --> 00:24:58.780
Then I go to sleep.

484
00:24:59.140 --> 00:25:01.600
When I wake up, I plug in the grill.

485
00:25:01.860 --> 00:25:03.220
I go back to sleep again.

486
00:25:03.840 --> 00:25:07.220
Then I wake up to the smell of crackling bacon.

487
00:25:07.820 --> 00:25:08.800
It is delicious.

488
00:25:08.800 --> 00:25:09.760
It's good for me.

489
00:25:09.960 --> 00:25:11.040
It's a perfect way to start the day.

490
00:25:12.220 --> 00:25:13.160
Today, I got up.

491
00:25:13.300 --> 00:25:16.460
I stepped onto the grill and I clamped down on my foot.

492
00:25:16.620 --> 00:25:16.960
That's it.

493
00:25:17.080 --> 00:25:19.380
I don't see what's so hard to believe about that.

494
00:25:22.850 --> 00:25:24.590
I love the big sigh.

495
00:25:24.830 --> 00:25:29.510
After you've explained how you eat bacon, six strips of bacon every morning, you're like,

496
00:25:30.390 --> 00:25:31.210
so today.

497
00:25:31.910 --> 00:25:39.780
I think my favorite part of that was when he says, and it's good for me.

498
00:25:40.800 --> 00:25:42.280
Just slipping in.

499
00:25:43.200 --> 00:25:47.940
How good, how healthy eating six strips of bacon in the morning.

500
00:25:48.760 --> 00:25:49.320
Oh.

501
00:25:49.820 --> 00:25:52.340
But there's so much about that if you unpack it.

502
00:25:52.460 --> 00:25:55.920
Like, it actually is really nice to wake up to the smell of bacon.

503
00:25:56.200 --> 00:25:58.200
I have that memory from childhood.

504
00:25:58.520 --> 00:25:58.800
It's comfort.

505
00:25:58.800 --> 00:26:02.460
You wake up on a Sunday, dad's downstairs making the pancakes,

506
00:26:02.460 --> 00:26:03.680
making some bacon.

507
00:26:04.280 --> 00:26:06.580
It is actually a really, he has a point.

508
00:26:07.060 --> 00:26:08.480
Maybe Jeff made bacon.

509
00:26:08.680 --> 00:26:10.860
He doesn't say it the way you just did, though.

510
00:26:11.220 --> 00:26:12.200
That's the problem.

511
00:26:12.860 --> 00:26:19.960
And what was so great about those talking heads and so terrifying in a way,

512
00:26:20.080 --> 00:26:25.280
it was just, they were so well written that you just didn't want to screw it up.

513
00:26:25.480 --> 00:26:28.460
You didn't, you, there was an obligation.

514
00:26:28.460 --> 00:26:30.560
And I know you guys felt this too.

515
00:26:30.780 --> 00:26:32.800
Like, when you had a really good line,

516
00:26:33.880 --> 00:26:39.880
and it here comes, you would try to do everything you could to take the pedal off a little bit.

517
00:26:39.940 --> 00:26:42.960
You know, don't, don't floor it for the joke.

518
00:26:43.360 --> 00:26:50.520
Really ease off and let it seem like this is just a natural extension of what this guy is thinking or what these women are thinking.

519
00:26:50.520 --> 00:26:56.260
And that was the hardest part, is to not point to all of these fantastic jokes,

520
00:26:56.260 --> 00:27:00.820
to just make them feel conversational, and also just to not laugh.

521
00:27:01.220 --> 00:27:06.280
I'm sure I laughed 20 times during that, trying to do that talking head.

522
00:27:06.540 --> 00:27:13.140
There's one talking head, I, what was it, that I could barely get through.

523
00:27:13.140 --> 00:27:14.920
And it was the same sort of thing.

524
00:27:14.920 --> 00:27:17.340
It was something so ridiculous.

525
00:27:17.660 --> 00:27:19.140
There's one in the bloopers.

526
00:27:19.460 --> 00:27:21.080
You know, Isabel's obsessed with the bloopers.

527
00:27:21.360 --> 00:27:21.940
She loves them.

528
00:27:22.120 --> 00:27:24.700
So she'll be like, oh, mom, you're at season seven.

529
00:27:24.880 --> 00:27:25.740
There's great bloopers.

530
00:27:25.920 --> 00:27:26.720
I'm like, okay, yes.

531
00:27:27.140 --> 00:27:30.880
But there was one, Steve, that you could not get through where you said,

532
00:27:31.380 --> 00:27:32.280
I'm going to get it wrong.

533
00:27:32.380 --> 00:27:34.880
But you're like, Michael's like, I'm not a bad guy.

534
00:27:35.060 --> 00:27:36.280
Sometimes I hit people with my car.

535
00:27:36.440 --> 00:27:36.980
That's the one.

536
00:27:37.120 --> 00:27:38.100
That's the one I was thinking.

537
00:27:38.420 --> 00:27:39.740
Yeah, you couldn't get through it.

538
00:27:39.820 --> 00:27:41.040
Sometimes I hit people with my car.

539
00:27:41.580 --> 00:27:43.640
Yeah, when I hit Meredith with my car.

540
00:27:44.920 --> 00:27:47.320
Trying to absolve myself of having done it.

541
00:27:47.420 --> 00:27:48.980
I'm not a bad guy sometimes.

542
00:27:49.480 --> 00:27:51.800
I just hit people with my car.

543
00:27:52.020 --> 00:27:54.760
And I remember doing it.

544
00:27:54.980 --> 00:27:57.100
And I'd be getting close.

545
00:27:57.440 --> 00:27:57.680
Yeah.

546
00:27:58.140 --> 00:28:01.240
And I couldn't, I couldn't get through it.

547
00:28:01.420 --> 00:28:06.540
It was so, it was, it's fun going to know you're going to go into work every day

548
00:28:06.540 --> 00:28:08.200
and laugh until you cry.

549
00:28:08.600 --> 00:28:08.840
Yeah.

550
00:28:08.980 --> 00:28:13.660
Every, every day I was on that set at one, at least one point of the day,

551
00:28:14.160 --> 00:28:16.760
I was laughing until tears were coming out of my eyes.

552
00:28:16.940 --> 00:28:18.300
And I mean, what a gift.

553
00:28:18.460 --> 00:28:18.760
Yes.

554
00:28:18.900 --> 00:28:19.040
Yeah.

555
00:28:19.380 --> 00:28:20.800
Who has a job like that?

556
00:28:21.740 --> 00:28:26.120
So Michael Scott had a lot of characters that he would play over the years.

557
00:28:26.360 --> 00:28:30.340
And we found a deleted scene, Steve, that I wanted to share with you.

558
00:28:30.440 --> 00:28:31.500
I don't know if you'll remember it.

559
00:28:31.620 --> 00:28:35.340
I, you know, when you hear it, you'll remember it, but it's from season three in The Convict.

560
00:28:36.120 --> 00:28:41.000
Michael explains where he got the idea, or I guess, inspiration for all of his characters,

561
00:28:41.280 --> 00:28:42.700
in particular, Prison Mike.

562
00:28:43.020 --> 00:28:44.620
I really wanted you to hear it.

563
00:28:44.860 --> 00:28:45.920
Sam, can you play it?

564
00:28:46.920 --> 00:28:57.900
When I was just a little kid, we had an assembly at school where a giant owl came out and gave

565
00:28:57.900 --> 00:29:06.760
a very impassioned speech about giving hoots and not polluting.

566
00:29:07.220 --> 00:29:08.380
And you know what?

567
00:29:08.380 --> 00:29:10.900
I never polluted again.

568
00:29:11.560 --> 00:29:17.440
It was right then that I realized the power of saying things as a character.

569
00:29:18.120 --> 00:29:30.140
People listen to you when you are wearing an elaborate costume or speaking in a voice that

570
00:29:30.140 --> 00:29:31.040
is not yours.

571
00:29:31.040 --> 00:29:36.230
Can you believe that was deleted?

572
00:29:36.730 --> 00:29:37.090
Oh my God.

573
00:29:37.130 --> 00:29:38.710
That explains Prison Mike.

574
00:29:39.170 --> 00:29:40.470
It explains everybody.

575
00:29:40.830 --> 00:29:46.130
Michael Scarn, anyone, anytime you led a conference room meeting in a costume with

576
00:29:46.130 --> 00:29:46.730
a voice.

577
00:29:46.930 --> 00:29:47.930
You thought, this is it?

578
00:29:47.990 --> 00:29:48.810
They're going to hear me.

579
00:29:48.830 --> 00:29:50.690
This is how I am most effective.

580
00:29:50.690 --> 00:29:51.110
How I get them.

581
00:29:51.350 --> 00:29:53.370
Because you don't give any hoots anymore.

582
00:29:54.410 --> 00:29:56.570
You give a hoot, you don't pollute.

583
00:29:56.690 --> 00:30:02.930
Clearly, this relates back to me tearing apart the plastic, you know, or the dolphins.

584
00:30:02.930 --> 00:30:03.750
Even still.

585
00:30:03.990 --> 00:30:05.330
You don't pollute.

586
00:30:05.470 --> 00:30:07.010
Steve Carell gave a hoot.

587
00:30:07.250 --> 00:30:08.290
Yeah, he sure did.

588
00:30:10.590 --> 00:30:17.610
Boy, I mean, that's sort of like that vague, foggy, as I was hearing it, I kind of recalled.

589
00:30:18.070 --> 00:30:18.310
Yeah.

590
00:30:18.810 --> 00:30:21.290
But there are thousands of those.

591
00:30:21.450 --> 00:30:28.150
There are like different versions of all of this stuff and things that could have made,

592
00:30:28.370 --> 00:30:31.190
you know, could made a difference either way.

593
00:30:31.350 --> 00:30:31.610
Yeah.

594
00:30:31.750 --> 00:30:32.490
But they were so good.

595
00:30:32.490 --> 00:30:38.310
You know, the editors and Greg, I never regretted anything that was taken out of the show.

596
00:30:38.550 --> 00:30:42.810
I like had complete confidence that, because you know how some people get possessive, like,

597
00:30:42.930 --> 00:30:44.070
well, why did that scene go?

598
00:30:44.270 --> 00:30:44.430
Right, right.

599
00:30:44.430 --> 00:30:45.490
Or what happened there?

600
00:30:45.490 --> 00:30:51.990
They were so good about structuring the show and what needed to be in and what was might

601
00:30:51.990 --> 00:30:54.590
have been really funny, but didn't help.

602
00:30:54.930 --> 00:30:54.990
Right.

603
00:30:54.990 --> 00:30:57.810
Or might have explained a whole backstory.

604
00:30:58.330 --> 00:31:01.430
But they just, they let the audience just jump in the middle.

605
00:31:01.690 --> 00:31:03.450
They didn't have to like explain everything.

606
00:31:03.630 --> 00:31:07.730
But every once in a while, as we're doing this rewatch and we find a deleted scene or something

607
00:31:07.730 --> 00:31:13.950
that's such a nugget, we're like, oh, that's why he would come in these costumes and give

608
00:31:13.950 --> 00:31:14.790
a big presentation.

609
00:31:14.850 --> 00:31:16.170
It's because of Hoot.

610
00:31:16.290 --> 00:31:17.010
Hoot the owl.

611
00:31:17.590 --> 00:31:19.950
Did you get to give notes on things?

612
00:31:20.210 --> 00:31:22.270
Like, were you very much a part of that?

613
00:31:22.610 --> 00:31:26.130
Like, I would remember sometimes you would stay behind after a table read and they would

614
00:31:26.130 --> 00:31:28.350
want to know your thoughts and things.

615
00:31:28.570 --> 00:31:30.970
They were really collaborative and open in that way, right?

616
00:31:31.150 --> 00:31:31.550
For sure.

617
00:31:31.770 --> 00:31:32.210
Yes.

618
00:31:32.410 --> 00:31:37.510
And the fact that so many of the writers were also actors on the show, there was such a

619
00:31:37.510 --> 00:31:42.570
great connection that it didn't feel like different facets of the show.

620
00:31:42.570 --> 00:31:47.890
So again, everybody just felt like they were all collaborators and the writers were part

621
00:31:47.890 --> 00:31:53.770
of the ensemble, which I think helped because they understood everyone's voice that much

622
00:31:53.770 --> 00:31:56.230
better because they were there and they were hearing it.

623
00:31:56.490 --> 00:31:58.710
But yeah, I mean, I'd weigh in.

624
00:31:58.910 --> 00:32:00.070
Would you pitch ideas?

625
00:32:00.390 --> 00:32:00.710
Sure.

626
00:32:00.930 --> 00:32:01.110
Yeah.

627
00:32:01.230 --> 00:32:02.030
Do you remember any?

628
00:32:02.610 --> 00:32:09.130
Well, I remember one specifically, and this was later on, this was when you and Jim were

629
00:32:09.130 --> 00:32:13.870
getting married and they wanted to have a horse go over Niagara Falls.

630
00:32:13.890 --> 00:32:14.730
Oh my gosh.

631
00:32:14.830 --> 00:32:23.210
And I, I, I raised my hand and said, we can't, that's, I mean, all due respect, but please

632
00:32:23.210 --> 00:32:23.910
don't do that.

633
00:32:25.650 --> 00:32:27.230
It was not just you.

634
00:32:27.610 --> 00:32:31.170
Apparently when we were breaking down that episode, we talked to Greg about it and he

635
00:32:31.170 --> 00:32:33.590
was like, Steve told me not to do it.

636
00:32:34.030 --> 00:32:35.750
Randy told me not to do it.

637
00:32:36.030 --> 00:32:37.710
You told me not to do it.

638
00:32:37.710 --> 00:32:42.110
Like literally everyone told him it was so hard for him to let go of that.

639
00:32:43.270 --> 00:32:47.030
I'm sure if it was Greg, it would have been funny.

640
00:32:47.410 --> 00:32:47.730
Definitely.

641
00:32:48.470 --> 00:32:56.430
But, and, and those were rare moments when you felt like, no, I, yeah, I mean, little

642
00:32:56.430 --> 00:33:02.930
notes here and there, but again, that those writers were so good and so on point about

643
00:33:02.930 --> 00:33:08.710
everything that it was more in addition to, as opposed to negating.

644
00:33:09.090 --> 00:33:09.210
Yeah.

645
00:33:10.010 --> 00:33:10.630
Side note.

646
00:33:10.850 --> 00:33:12.710
Did you know, we didn't know this.

647
00:33:13.150 --> 00:33:16.150
Randy shared this with us that up until like, right?

648
00:33:16.370 --> 00:33:21.550
Like the day before they had a horse, they were training in a big water pool, Paramount

649
00:33:21.550 --> 00:33:22.130
Studios.

650
00:33:22.630 --> 00:33:26.650
Like it was, they were all systems go like right up until like the day before.

651
00:33:26.830 --> 00:33:28.970
There was a horse being trained.

652
00:33:29.190 --> 00:33:29.950
Of course there was.

653
00:33:30.250 --> 00:33:34.910
That's what you don't realize when you write something, somebody starts to build it.

654
00:33:35.090 --> 00:33:35.230
Yeah.

655
00:33:35.410 --> 00:33:36.830
I mean, it starts to happen.

656
00:33:37.070 --> 00:33:42.610
So when you say, oh, so now we're going to be out in the woods.

657
00:33:43.590 --> 00:33:49.370
And then a month later, you're driving an hour and a half up into the Los Angeles forest

658
00:33:49.370 --> 00:33:51.050
to shoot a few scenes.

659
00:33:51.230 --> 00:33:52.690
Like, why did I write this?

660
00:33:53.590 --> 00:33:54.470
Terrible idea.

661
00:33:55.070 --> 00:33:57.890
It's nighttime in the forest, the middle of nowhere.

662
00:33:58.850 --> 00:33:59.290
Freezing.

663
00:33:59.630 --> 00:34:00.070
Terrible.

664
00:34:00.490 --> 00:34:01.790
He falls in a creek.

665
00:34:01.970 --> 00:34:02.210
What?

666
00:34:03.030 --> 00:34:04.410
Why did I write it?

667
00:34:04.790 --> 00:34:07.510
So we've talked how the show was slow to find an audience.

668
00:34:07.510 --> 00:34:10.870
It was sort of like we were doing a little theater production and no one's seeing it.

669
00:34:10.870 --> 00:34:15.950
But then one day it turned a corner and everybody knew the show and we had people coming up

670
00:34:15.950 --> 00:34:16.250
to us.

671
00:34:16.350 --> 00:34:20.410
Jenna, do you remember we're at an award show and Laura Dern was like, stop, I have to talk

672
00:34:20.410 --> 00:34:20.730
to you.

673
00:34:20.810 --> 00:34:21.250
And we're like, what?

674
00:34:21.570 --> 00:34:22.750
She was like, I love your show.

675
00:34:22.830 --> 00:34:24.370
And I was like, this is Laura Dern.

676
00:34:24.590 --> 00:34:25.290
Oh my gosh.

677
00:34:25.470 --> 00:34:29.670
But was there anyone like that for you, Steve, that sort of surprised you and caught you

678
00:34:29.670 --> 00:34:31.630
off guard that was a fan of the show?

679
00:34:32.070 --> 00:34:32.730
Laura Dern.

680
00:34:33.550 --> 00:34:34.170
Same thing.

681
00:34:34.510 --> 00:34:36.990
I met her somewhere down the road.

682
00:34:37.130 --> 00:34:39.890
I was doing a promotional thing with her.

683
00:34:40.730 --> 00:34:46.410
And I wasn't even aware of your encounter with her because she was clearly a fan because

684
00:34:46.410 --> 00:34:49.310
she was very excited to talk about the show.

685
00:34:49.530 --> 00:34:51.390
And, you know, she said the same things to me.

686
00:34:51.510 --> 00:34:52.090
She loved it.

687
00:34:52.230 --> 00:34:58.190
She was a, and that's, it's nice when, you know, it's nice when anyone likes something

688
00:34:58.190 --> 00:34:59.030
that you're a part of.

689
00:34:59.030 --> 00:35:05.470
And especially nice when somebody that you respect like that and has sort of a critical

690
00:35:05.470 --> 00:35:07.710
eye for things likes your show.

691
00:35:07.890 --> 00:35:09.050
So that, yeah, it was really nice.

692
00:35:09.210 --> 00:35:13.350
It was nice hearing any of it, you know, because you, you're right.

693
00:35:13.550 --> 00:35:15.490
Early on, it felt like we were in a vacuum.

694
00:35:16.530 --> 00:35:20.430
And is any, let alone appreciating, is anyone watching it?

695
00:35:20.590 --> 00:35:23.530
Is anyone, you know, paying any sort of attention?

696
00:35:23.770 --> 00:35:28.050
And the only part of it that mattered to me was the continuation of the show.

697
00:35:28.050 --> 00:35:34.650
I wanted to have enough people watch it so they kept letting us do it because I didn't

698
00:35:34.650 --> 00:35:40.930
really care about the acclaim, you know, or like we knew that we liked it and we were

699
00:35:40.930 --> 00:35:41.730
having fun with it.

700
00:35:41.810 --> 00:35:45.810
And I think we all felt like it was special in that moment.

701
00:35:45.830 --> 00:35:47.610
And that's what mattered to me.

702
00:35:48.070 --> 00:35:51.110
But yeah, yeah, it was Laura Dern.

703
00:35:51.110 --> 00:35:58.150
I remember that time early on because we, a few of us had gotten on MySpace and we were

704
00:35:58.150 --> 00:36:03.030
able to interact with the like 17 people who were watching our show in the beginning.

705
00:36:03.290 --> 00:36:07.130
And they were such fans and they so got it and they so appreciated it.

706
00:36:07.210 --> 00:36:10.930
And that really like kind of, that just felt so good, you know, because when you make a

707
00:36:10.930 --> 00:36:15.950
television show, you don't hear the people laugh when they watch it and you don't know

708
00:36:16.390 --> 00:36:20.450
how it's being received or a movie or whatever, I guess, unless you sneak in the theater,

709
00:36:20.450 --> 00:36:23.330
but you can't like go sneak in people's living rooms while they watch it.

710
00:36:23.650 --> 00:36:29.450
So I always loved when someone told us that they loved it, especially a fellow artiSt.

711
00:36:30.050 --> 00:36:35.350
And they were excited too, because the show was different with the documentary style and

712
00:36:35.350 --> 00:36:36.370
the looking at the camera.

713
00:36:36.510 --> 00:36:39.410
Would you look into the camera after you got jobs on the office?

714
00:36:39.550 --> 00:36:43.630
I have never had a job where I didn't look into the camera at some point.

715
00:36:44.050 --> 00:36:44.450
Same.

716
00:36:44.710 --> 00:36:47.370
I can't not make the camera a character now.

717
00:36:47.510 --> 00:36:48.990
It's so, such a habit.

718
00:36:48.990 --> 00:36:50.310
At least once.

719
00:36:50.570 --> 00:36:50.690
Yeah.

720
00:36:51.110 --> 00:36:56.490
I'm sure on Foxcatcher, I like looked into the camera and went, nope, you cannot comment

721
00:36:56.490 --> 00:36:58.390
on what you just did.

722
00:36:58.590 --> 00:36:59.030
Yeah.

723
00:36:59.210 --> 00:37:00.850
Because that's not this show.

724
00:37:01.350 --> 00:37:01.490
Yeah.

725
00:37:01.930 --> 00:37:02.730
Same for me.

726
00:37:02.870 --> 00:37:06.110
I actually had a director say, Angela, you just looked right down the barrel.

727
00:37:06.750 --> 00:37:08.250
I was like, oh, I'm sorry.

728
00:37:09.010 --> 00:37:10.470
I looked to the left or right.

729
00:37:10.750 --> 00:37:14.010
It was such a fun kind of discipline.

730
00:37:14.210 --> 00:37:17.430
Know who was really good at that right off the bat was Amy AdaMs.

731
00:37:17.430 --> 00:37:22.930
I'll never forget, because it was kind of a learned strength, I think, on the show, to

732
00:37:22.930 --> 00:37:29.710
figure out the right way to play the camera, to invite the camera in, and to make it feel

733
00:37:29.710 --> 00:37:32.830
like these people aren't used to the cameras.

734
00:37:33.030 --> 00:37:36.610
So you couldn't be too savvy, especially at firSt.

735
00:37:36.610 --> 00:37:38.850
You couldn't play it too well.

736
00:37:38.850 --> 00:37:44.670
Well, you needed to have an awareness of it without having an over-awareness of it.

737
00:37:44.810 --> 00:37:45.030
Yes.

738
00:37:45.670 --> 00:37:49.350
But I remember when Amy Adams came in to do Purse Girl.

739
00:37:49.350 --> 00:37:49.950
Was that it?

740
00:37:50.390 --> 00:37:51.110
Hot Girl.

741
00:37:51.250 --> 00:37:51.710
Hot Girl.

742
00:37:51.930 --> 00:37:52.470
She was selling purses.

743
00:37:52.470 --> 00:37:53.150
Selling the purses.

744
00:37:53.230 --> 00:37:53.830
Selling purses, yeah.

745
00:37:53.830 --> 00:38:00.190
And I remember she just had like this, she was right up to speed with everybody else in

746
00:38:00.190 --> 00:38:01.350
terms of how to play the camera.

747
00:38:01.510 --> 00:38:03.230
Like, it's eavesdropping.

748
00:38:03.250 --> 00:38:07.310
I don't know how I should react to it.

749
00:38:07.630 --> 00:38:12.290
And that sort of discomfort built in that we had learned how to do.

750
00:38:12.670 --> 00:38:14.170
So she was really good at that.

751
00:38:14.290 --> 00:38:18.170
And that was such an interesting, and we'll never get to do something like that again.

752
00:38:18.170 --> 00:38:23.770
It was a really interesting acting exercise to be, you know, you're doing, you're in your

753
00:38:23.770 --> 00:38:28.850
character, and your character is also being watched.

754
00:38:29.330 --> 00:38:34.310
So there was like another layer of something going on with all of these characters, which

755
00:38:34.310 --> 00:38:34.870
is kind of cool.

756
00:38:35.470 --> 00:38:36.870
You mentioned Amy AdaMs.

757
00:38:37.230 --> 00:38:41.710
One of my favorite guest stars that we had in terms of chemistry with you was Tim Meadows.

758
00:38:42.230 --> 00:38:44.490
You guys doing the baby back ribs.

759
00:38:44.490 --> 00:38:49.210
Yes, that also, that episode has one of my favorite lines in it, which is, Gould is dead

760
00:38:50.010 --> 00:38:52.790
because Jan is no longer Jan Levinson Gould.

761
00:38:53.070 --> 00:38:53.330
Yeah.

762
00:38:53.470 --> 00:38:56.190
And she's like, no, Michael, we got, we're separated.

763
00:38:56.350 --> 00:38:56.990
We're divorced.

764
00:38:57.230 --> 00:38:57.990
Gould didn't die.

765
00:38:58.110 --> 00:39:04.470
As if, as if she would drop the Gould from her name if he died, right, as a widow.

766
00:39:04.850 --> 00:39:05.250
Yeah.

767
00:39:05.530 --> 00:39:05.910
Amazing.

768
00:39:06.370 --> 00:39:08.510
But yeah, I love you guys.

769
00:39:08.690 --> 00:39:13.930
I don't know how you got through all the rib eating and singing and comedy.

770
00:39:14.490 --> 00:39:15.350
From that episode.

771
00:39:15.490 --> 00:39:17.710
I've known Tim for a long time too.

772
00:39:17.930 --> 00:39:22.790
And we did a Second City show that went to Washington, D.C.

773
00:39:23.090 --> 00:39:25.190
at the Kennedy Center with Nancy.

774
00:39:25.490 --> 00:39:31.630
We, we went as this, this group one summer and spent, I don't know, three, three weeks

775
00:39:31.630 --> 00:39:34.190
at the Kennedy Center doing a Second City show.

776
00:39:34.190 --> 00:39:38.830
And I had known him from Second City as well, you know, earlier on stage.

777
00:39:38.990 --> 00:39:46.350
And he is so, I think he's one of those, one of those people that is always great.

778
00:39:46.630 --> 00:39:48.470
He's never not great.

779
00:39:48.910 --> 00:39:54.130
You know, sometimes people like that are under the radar and then they pop out and you say,

780
00:39:54.130 --> 00:39:57.250
oh my gosh, like, they're incredible.

781
00:39:58.130 --> 00:40:02.430
And yeah, he's, I think, one of the best and he's a great improviser.

782
00:40:02.710 --> 00:40:05.870
And he's so, he's so effortless.

783
00:40:05.990 --> 00:40:10.670
He just doesn't look like he's ever breaking a sweat and he's hilarious.

784
00:40:11.690 --> 00:40:12.930
Yeah, I'm a big fan.

785
00:40:13.030 --> 00:40:13.650
He's a great guy.

786
00:40:14.930 --> 00:40:16.610
Well, why don't we take a break?

787
00:40:16.610 --> 00:40:19.650
And when we come back, we have some more questions from the fans.

788
00:40:19.890 --> 00:40:20.170
Okie do.

789
00:40:20.170 --> 00:40:30.140
All right, we're back.

790
00:40:30.280 --> 00:40:34.200
And while we're on break, before we get to fan questions, Steve, I remembered this cold

791
00:40:34.200 --> 00:40:39.660
open that we did that led into a talking head that was one sentence from you.

792
00:40:40.160 --> 00:40:42.720
When I rewatched it, I laughed so hard.

793
00:40:42.860 --> 00:40:44.580
I had tears streaming down my face.

794
00:40:44.920 --> 00:40:45.100
What?

795
00:40:45.460 --> 00:40:50.460
Do you remember the cold open that we did where we're all trying to take the Christmas photo?

796
00:40:51.040 --> 00:40:51.520
Yes.

797
00:40:52.080 --> 00:40:52.560
Yes.

798
00:40:52.940 --> 00:40:57.740
And we're trying to jump on three, three, two, one, one, two, three.

799
00:40:58.060 --> 00:40:58.260
Yeah.

800
00:40:58.260 --> 00:40:59.360
This whole thing.

801
00:40:59.500 --> 00:41:01.100
Dwight's like, Phyllis didn't jump.

802
00:41:01.260 --> 00:41:02.140
Angela didn't jump.

803
00:41:02.300 --> 00:41:02.500
Yes.

804
00:41:03.080 --> 00:41:05.300
And it's so long, this cold open.

805
00:41:05.720 --> 00:41:08.200
And then it cuts to a talking head of Michael.

806
00:41:08.400 --> 00:41:10.540
And Michael says, we never got it.

807
00:41:12.350 --> 00:41:13.370
I, you know what?

808
00:41:13.530 --> 00:41:20.610
That, that cold open is a perfect demonstration of us as an ensemble, I think.

809
00:41:20.830 --> 00:41:20.990
Yeah.

810
00:41:20.990 --> 00:41:28.730
Because to, to make it look as bad as it did, took some effort.

811
00:41:29.010 --> 00:41:29.250
It did.

812
00:41:29.450 --> 00:41:32.810
Like, we had to be completely in sync to be that out of sync.

813
00:41:33.010 --> 00:41:33.230
Yeah.

814
00:41:33.230 --> 00:41:36.330
And everyone jumping at different times.

815
00:41:36.610 --> 00:41:36.770
Yeah.

816
00:41:37.330 --> 00:41:39.550
And Michael getting frustrated like a dad would.

817
00:41:39.670 --> 00:41:40.630
Like, come on, come on, guys.

818
00:41:40.630 --> 00:41:45.570
It, I think that's a perfect example of, of how in sync we were as a caSt.

819
00:41:45.690 --> 00:41:47.670
Because we all got what that thing was.

820
00:41:47.870 --> 00:41:48.030
Yeah.

821
00:41:48.110 --> 00:41:50.190
And how many people needed to be going at one time.

822
00:41:50.350 --> 00:41:51.750
But we didn't talk about it.

823
00:41:51.830 --> 00:41:52.070
No.

824
00:41:52.210 --> 00:41:55.970
We didn't discuss like, well, on the count of three, you two are going to go.

825
00:41:55.970 --> 00:41:59.650
And then we just, as a group, we just felt that.

826
00:41:59.850 --> 00:42:01.890
And not to give ourselves too big a pat on the back.

827
00:42:02.030 --> 00:42:07.830
But, but I think I've, I've watched that since and thought the same thing.

828
00:42:07.970 --> 00:42:11.450
Like that's, there was something, something cool happening there.

829
00:42:11.610 --> 00:42:14.750
That was one of my favorite group cold opens for that reason.

830
00:42:15.070 --> 00:42:15.450
I know.

831
00:42:15.690 --> 00:42:17.530
Well, let's get into these fan questions.

832
00:42:18.270 --> 00:42:23.090
Catherine G and Christy R both asked if you had to choose a different character to play on The Office.

833
00:42:23.290 --> 00:42:25.530
Besides Michael, is there one you'd like to be?

834
00:42:25.530 --> 00:42:26.230
Wow.

835
00:42:26.570 --> 00:42:30.330
I can't imagine anyone but the people who played their parts.

836
00:42:30.870 --> 00:42:35.630
Well, right off the top of my head, I think Dwight is a very fun role to play.

837
00:42:35.990 --> 00:42:39.870
But I would have been terrible.

838
00:42:40.330 --> 00:42:42.610
I mean, Rain was perfect for that part.

839
00:42:43.890 --> 00:42:47.670
So no, I never, like, I never imagined myself in another part.

840
00:42:48.010 --> 00:42:49.990
It was, it was just as it should have been.

841
00:42:50.710 --> 00:42:52.030
Lauren D asked,

842
00:42:52.030 --> 00:42:59.750
First, what trend or big pop culture moment that's happened since 2011, do you think Michael would have gotten obsessed about?

843
00:43:00.090 --> 00:43:04.490
For example, Lauren thinks Michael would have been all over the ice bucket challenge.

844
00:43:04.670 --> 00:43:05.330
Oh, yeah.

845
00:43:05.330 --> 00:43:05.930
In 2014.

846
00:43:06.250 --> 00:43:08.150
I think, I think she's absolutely right.

847
00:43:08.250 --> 00:43:09.010
So much, so much ice water.

848
00:43:09.190 --> 00:43:13.010
Oh, and would have, would have done something wrong.

849
00:43:13.430 --> 00:43:15.490
Someone might have ended up in the hospital.

850
00:43:16.270 --> 00:43:17.130
Probably Meredith.

851
00:43:18.050 --> 00:43:18.570
Yeah.

852
00:43:18.770 --> 00:43:20.390
Dwight would have overdone it too.

853
00:43:21.070 --> 00:43:21.470
Yes.

854
00:43:23.150 --> 00:43:23.550
Yeah.

855
00:43:23.690 --> 00:43:25.290
It would have gone south somehow.

856
00:43:25.490 --> 00:43:26.310
It would have gone wrong.

857
00:43:26.630 --> 00:43:30.890
We say that we think that Michael probably would have made us all learn TikTok dances.

858
00:43:31.450 --> 00:43:31.810
Oh, sure.

859
00:43:31.810 --> 00:43:33.050
So many TikTok dances.

860
00:43:33.370 --> 00:43:33.510
Yeah.

861
00:43:33.730 --> 00:43:39.350
Well, the internet wasn't as certainly as, as much of a thing.

862
00:43:40.010 --> 00:43:41.210
Social media really.

863
00:43:41.610 --> 00:43:41.930
Yeah.

864
00:43:41.930 --> 00:43:44.290
We had, we had flip phones when we started the show.

865
00:43:44.390 --> 00:43:45.530
Then we got BlackBerrys.

866
00:43:45.650 --> 00:43:46.930
And then at the end, we had iPhones.

867
00:43:47.250 --> 00:43:47.350
Like.

868
00:43:47.590 --> 00:43:47.850
Yeah.

869
00:43:48.070 --> 00:43:48.470
Yeah.

870
00:43:48.630 --> 00:43:49.770
A lot, a lot changed.

871
00:43:49.770 --> 00:43:50.090
Yeah.

872
00:43:50.270 --> 00:43:55.770
There are, there are tons of things that Michael would have glommed onto in, in the

873
00:43:55.770 --> 00:43:56.830
worst possible way.

874
00:43:59.350 --> 00:44:05.210
Beth M writes in and says, what is the most common Michael line that people quote back

875
00:44:05.210 --> 00:44:05.650
to you?

876
00:44:05.770 --> 00:44:06.970
What do you think it is?

877
00:44:06.970 --> 00:44:07.570
Yeah, Beth.

878
00:44:07.770 --> 00:44:09.130
That's what she said.

879
00:44:10.410 --> 00:44:12.490
Is there one other than that?

880
00:44:15.860 --> 00:44:18.940
That's the one, that's the one, that's the one I hear.

881
00:44:19.260 --> 00:44:19.500
Yeah.

882
00:44:19.500 --> 00:44:24.760
And sometimes, I don't know, do people ever stop you and say, oh, could you talk to, can

883
00:44:24.760 --> 00:44:27.500
you do a shout out, you know, into my phone?

884
00:44:27.680 --> 00:44:29.380
And, and I'm happy to do it.

885
00:44:29.440 --> 00:44:31.720
But when they say, can you say that?

886
00:44:31.720 --> 00:44:34.540
I can't, I just, I don't, I can't.

887
00:44:34.640 --> 00:44:36.660
It can be taken out of context.

888
00:44:37.020 --> 00:44:39.280
Please don't make Steve say that's what she said.

889
00:44:39.460 --> 00:44:40.920
If you see him at the airport.

890
00:44:41.600 --> 00:44:44.360
Do you ever, that's what she said in your life.

891
00:44:44.360 --> 00:44:45.440
Do you ever find?

892
00:44:45.740 --> 00:44:46.000
Never.

893
00:44:46.240 --> 00:44:46.620
No.

894
00:44:46.940 --> 00:44:47.320
Really?

895
00:44:47.540 --> 00:44:48.200
I don't do it.

896
00:44:48.300 --> 00:44:50.080
I, I do throw them out.

897
00:44:50.240 --> 00:44:50.600
Jenna does.

898
00:44:50.960 --> 00:44:51.480
I do.

899
00:44:51.860 --> 00:44:52.180
Yeah.

900
00:44:52.420 --> 00:44:54.880
Well, you can, you, you can.

901
00:44:55.180 --> 00:44:57.400
Gosh, it's gonna be so weird when we give you your parting gift.

902
00:44:57.520 --> 00:44:59.460
It's a, it's a t-shirt that says that's what she said.

903
00:45:00.040 --> 00:45:00.420
Wow.

904
00:45:00.820 --> 00:45:01.540
You'll love it.

905
00:45:01.600 --> 00:45:02.740
I do wear that all the time.

906
00:45:02.740 --> 00:45:04.740
And then on the back it says, I'm fantastic.

907
00:45:05.900 --> 00:45:06.660
Same brain.

908
00:45:07.960 --> 00:45:13.180
We really liked this question because we both come from a long line of teachers.

909
00:45:13.540 --> 00:45:16.180
Someone wanted to know if you remember a favorite teacher.

910
00:45:16.500 --> 00:45:21.180
There was one in particular, a guy named Mr. Blackman, my second grade teacher.

911
00:45:22.480 --> 00:45:26.740
And he's the one who got me interested in acting in the first place.

912
00:45:26.960 --> 00:45:30.920
We were doing like a pilgrims meet the Native Americans thing.

913
00:45:31.140 --> 00:45:32.840
It was a long time ago.

914
00:45:33.060 --> 00:45:36.000
So a lot was being glossed over for sure.

915
00:45:36.000 --> 00:45:41.880
But what I remember is we were rowing canoes and it was all mime.

916
00:45:42.000 --> 00:45:44.560
You know, we were sitting in little chairs in the classroom.

917
00:45:44.760 --> 00:45:45.580
It wasn't a big play.

918
00:45:45.760 --> 00:45:47.160
It was just like an in-class thing.

919
00:45:48.140 --> 00:45:51.080
And I started rowing the canoe, my mime rowing.

920
00:45:51.220 --> 00:45:54.220
And then I changed my hands with the oar and rowed on the other side.

921
00:45:54.520 --> 00:45:55.580
I'll never forget it.

922
00:45:55.660 --> 00:45:56.740
He singled me out.

923
00:45:56.900 --> 00:45:58.900
It's the first time I'd ever been singled out in any way.

924
00:46:00.080 --> 00:46:02.900
And he gave me a pat on the back and said,

925
00:46:03.440 --> 00:46:07.480
notice how Steve rowed on both sides of the canoe so he wouldn't go in a circle.

926
00:46:07.800 --> 00:46:12.940
And he kept the canoe straight, you know, in this mime, you know, miming the canoe.

927
00:46:13.280 --> 00:46:15.920
And something about that really struck me.

928
00:46:16.060 --> 00:46:22.100
And I thought, and apart from that, he was just the most lovely guy and really kind to all the kids.

929
00:46:22.220 --> 00:46:23.400
And everyone, it was fun.

930
00:46:23.560 --> 00:46:25.160
It was really just a fun class.

931
00:46:25.160 --> 00:46:27.220
And he did that for all the kids, too.

932
00:46:27.340 --> 00:46:34.080
He made them feel special about whatever talent they had, whether it was art or music or anything.

933
00:46:34.480 --> 00:46:38.900
He really embraced, you know, everyone's abilities.

934
00:46:39.300 --> 00:46:44.220
You know, as little as they were in that moment as a second grader.

935
00:46:44.460 --> 00:46:45.320
But I'll never forget it.

936
00:46:45.440 --> 00:46:47.240
Like being seen in that way.

937
00:46:47.240 --> 00:46:47.900
Yeah, you're out-seen.

938
00:46:48.180 --> 00:46:48.440
Yeah.

939
00:46:48.580 --> 00:46:50.380
And it was, that was special.

940
00:46:50.480 --> 00:46:51.300
Something sort of clicked.

941
00:46:51.480 --> 00:46:52.820
Like, oh, that felt good.

942
00:46:53.460 --> 00:46:56.140
And so I guess I, I owe it all to him.

943
00:46:56.660 --> 00:46:59.720
You know, Steve, I love that story because I have a similar story.

944
00:46:59.900 --> 00:47:03.040
I took a summer school acting class and we had to do pantomime.

945
00:47:03.420 --> 00:47:04.800
We had to mime things.

946
00:47:04.960 --> 00:47:10.960
And my assignment was to go over to the refrigerator and get out some milk and pour it in a glass and drink it.

947
00:47:11.380 --> 00:47:12.580
And so I did it.

948
00:47:12.720 --> 00:47:18.540
And afterwards, the teacher said, did everyone notice how Jenna shut the refrigerator with her foot?

949
00:47:18.800 --> 00:47:19.160
Oh!

950
00:47:19.380 --> 00:47:20.560
Yeah, it's exactly it.

951
00:47:20.560 --> 00:47:24.340
And I was like, wait, I might be good at this because I didn't think.

952
00:47:24.920 --> 00:47:27.200
It just, I, maybe I have a little thing.

953
00:47:27.380 --> 00:47:28.040
Right, exactly.

954
00:47:28.180 --> 00:47:32.080
You know, and then it just makes you feel like I might be good at this.

955
00:47:32.260 --> 00:47:33.800
Or it might be something.

956
00:47:34.100 --> 00:47:34.280
Yeah.

957
00:47:34.400 --> 00:47:38.340
You know, it might be something that I'm actually, yeah, capable of.

958
00:47:38.540 --> 00:47:39.460
That's so fun.

959
00:47:39.460 --> 00:47:41.520
And it's not even like, look at me.

960
00:47:41.740 --> 00:47:42.020
No.

961
00:47:42.820 --> 00:47:48.260
It's just a sense of, like one of the first senses of pride that you ever feel.

962
00:47:48.420 --> 00:47:48.600
Yes.

963
00:47:48.720 --> 00:47:53.780
That you accomplish something completely on your own, not your parents and nothing that you were

964
00:47:53.780 --> 00:47:54.780
instructed to do.

965
00:47:54.880 --> 00:47:56.560
It's just something that you did naturally.

966
00:47:57.320 --> 00:48:01.160
And to have somebody notice it felt really good.

967
00:48:01.340 --> 00:48:03.480
And that's a great teacher in my mind.

968
00:48:03.700 --> 00:48:05.520
Somebody who can pick up on those things.

969
00:48:05.940 --> 00:48:06.120
Yeah.

970
00:48:06.660 --> 00:48:08.380
Okay, I love this question.

971
00:48:09.140 --> 00:48:09.920
Here it is.

972
00:48:10.060 --> 00:48:15.120
Are there any foods you don't eat anymore because of so many takes you had to do with

973
00:48:15.120 --> 00:48:16.180
that certain food?

974
00:48:16.180 --> 00:48:18.380
It can be on the office or any other job.

975
00:48:18.560 --> 00:48:19.840
I know mine right away.

976
00:48:20.020 --> 00:48:22.060
It's that cake that we had to eat.

977
00:48:22.180 --> 00:48:24.680
The ice cream cake for Meredith's birthday.

978
00:48:24.820 --> 00:48:25.860
Was it the Alliance?

979
00:48:26.180 --> 00:48:30.700
We started filming at 730 in the morning and we were given a slice of cake.

980
00:48:30.860 --> 00:48:33.500
And, you know, this is a rookie move on my part.

981
00:48:33.500 --> 00:48:38.140
I took a plate with a big piece of cake and I took a huge bite in that first take.

982
00:48:38.400 --> 00:48:43.400
And by the end, my fork was shaking as it got close to my mouth because I was like, I can't,

983
00:48:43.400 --> 00:48:44.860
I can't eat it anymore.

984
00:48:45.440 --> 00:48:47.080
Anyway, I was asking you that.

985
00:48:47.280 --> 00:48:51.620
But clearly I have a visceral, like, I can't eat ice cream cake anymore.

986
00:48:51.620 --> 00:48:52.640
That did it.

987
00:48:52.820 --> 00:48:53.360
That did you in.

988
00:48:53.560 --> 00:48:54.280
Yeah, it's so gross to me.

989
00:48:54.440 --> 00:48:56.800
I learned early on to nibble.

990
00:48:57.020 --> 00:48:57.420
Yeah.

991
00:48:57.960 --> 00:49:03.880
Not like if that first take, try not to take a big bite because then you have to match it

992
00:49:03.880 --> 00:49:05.440
